          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.12,
            0.353,
            0.141
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.145,
            0.617,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "1. How many whole units are there in $\\frac { 7 } { 2 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.191,
            0.708,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "2. How many whole units are there in $\\frac{4}{3}$ and in $\\frac { 7 } { 3 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.833,
            0.143,
            0.919,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.234,
            0.816,
            0.256
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Writing fractions greater than one as mixed numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.265,
            0.501,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "We saw that: ${ \\textstyle { \\frac { 3 } { 2 } }   =   1   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.302,
            0.878,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "We can write other fractions in a similar way. For example,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.293,
            0.324,
            0.565,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { 4 } { 3 } }   =   { \\underbrace { { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } }   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } }   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } } _ { } }   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } }   =   1   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } }   .",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.374,
            0.436,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "3 \\times { \\textstyle { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } } = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.412,
            0.352,
            0.434
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.441,
            0.884,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "1. Figure out the number of whole units in each of the following fractions: a. $\\frac{8}{3}$ b. $\\frac { 1 1 } { 5 }$ . $\\frac { 9 } { 4 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.531,
            0.349,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": "We saw that"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.554,
            0.483,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.536,
            0.552,
            0.881,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "This number is thus also called ‘two and two thirds’. We also write it as $2   \\frac { 2 } { 3 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.6,
            0.482,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": "Fraction Mixed number"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.634,
            0.883,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "2. Can all fractions greater than 1 be written as such mixed numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.68,
            0.861,
            0.742
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A mixed number or mixed fraction contains a whole number (called the whole part) and a fraction that is less than 1 (called the fractional part)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.771,
            0.841,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "Write the following fractions as mixed fractions (e.g., $\\textstyle { \\frac { 9 } { 2 } }   = 4   { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } )$ a. $\\frac { 9 } { 2 }$ b. $\\frac{9}{5}$ c. $\\frac { 2 1 } { 1 9 }$ d. $\\frac { 4 7 } { 9 }$ e. $\\frac { 1 2 } { 1 1 }$ f. $\\frac { 1 9 } { 6 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.087,
            0.941,
            0.115,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
